IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 891 of 2023
CITATION: 2024 SCC OnLine SC 2270
YEAR: 2024

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: B.R. Gavai, Sandeep Mehta

PARTIES:
Prem Prakash vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002, Indian Evidence Act, 1872

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 50 PMLA, Section 25 Evidence Act

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
confession to ed, admissibility of statements, custodial confession

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Clarified the evidentiary admissibility of statements recorded under Section 50 PMLA, affirming that statements procured while in police or judicial custody remain vulnerable to evidentiary exclusions.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 50 PMLA, Section 25 Evidence Act.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

